{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "aeci-api",
  "account_id": "004dc1af737b22a8aa83b3550fa9b9d3",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-10",
  // The API Worker is PRIVATE. It is reachable only via the SSR Worker's
  // `API` service binding (see `apps/web/wrangler.jsonc`). No `routes:` and
  // `workers_dev: false` for every published env so there is no public
  // ingress. Per docs/STAGE_1_SPEC.md §15.2 and docs/CICD_PLAN.md §4.2.
  //
  // The application database is Cloudflare D1, reached through the native `DB`
  // binding via Drizzle (ADR 0016). `nodejs_compat` is intentionally not enabled:
  // the DB path does not need it.
  //
  // Observability (ADR 0024) needs no secret: the publishable `phc_`
  // POSTHOG_PROJECT_KEY is a committed per-env var (AECI-640) and authenticates
  // all three PostHog intakes.
  //
  // Secrets (set via `wrangler secret put`):
  //   CF_ZONE_ID  — Cloudflare zone id for aecintegrations.com. Public value;
  //                 provision per env. Consumed (as the GraphQL zoneTag) by the
  //                 AECI-262 WAF firewall-event poll, paired with CF_ANALYTICS_API_TOKEN.
  //   CF_ANALYTICS_API_TOKEN — Cloudflare API token the hourly WAF firewall-event
  //                 poll presents to the GraphQL Analytics API to read the zone's
  //                 firewallEventsAdaptiveGroups and emit aeci.waf.ratelimit.blocked
  //                 (AECI-262 / §15.1). Scope: `Zone Analytics: Read` on
  //                 aecintegrations.com, a narrow read-only scope, so it's its own
  //                 secret. One un-suffixed GH
  //                 secret covers the shared zone across all envs; CI pushes it per
  //                 env (deploy.yml / promote-to-demo.yml / promote-to-prod.yml —
  //                 graceful warn-skip, no hard gate). Optional + fail-safe: absent →
  //                 the poll logs outcome:skipped_no_creds and no-ops. See
  //                 docs/waf-rate-limits.md §5.
  //   ALGOLIA_APP_ID    — Algolia app id (AECI-134). Shared across envs. Consumed at
  //                 runtime by the AECI-139 daily-sync cron + promote→index hook
  //                 (and the SSR search bootstrap). Pushed per env via `wrangler
  //                 secret put` in CI.
  //   ALGOLIA_ADMIN_KEY — per-env MANAGEMENT key (search + index-mutation ACLs,
  //                 scoped to this env's indexes; NOT the app-wide root admin key).
  //                 API/sync use only — never client-exposed. Provision per env via
  //                 `node scripts/algolia/provision.mjs`; see docs/CICD_PLAN.md §7.5.
  //                 Required on production (verify-worker-secrets.sh fails the deploy
  //                 if absent — AECI-139); absent on local → the cron +
  //                 promote hook are a graceful no-op.
  //   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY — Anthropic API key for review toxicity scoring via Claude
  //                 Haiku (AECI-258, supersedes the sunsetting Perspective API of
  //                 AECI-198). Read at runtime by `scoreToxicity()` on POST
  //                 /api/reviews. Optional + fail-open: absent (local) →
  //                 scoring is a silent no-op storing toxicity_score=null (emits no
  //                 metric); an outage stores null too. Never blocks a submission.
  //                 CI pushes ANTHROPIC_API_KEY_STAGING to staging and
  //                 ANTHROPIC_API_KEY_PRODUCTION to demo and production, as
  //                 recommended (no CI gate). GDPR prerequisite: the
  //                 Messages API has no per-request no-store control, so the
  //                 Anthropic org behind a real key MUST have zero data retention
  //                 (ZDR) enabled or scored review bodies are retained ~30 days
  //                 outside the AUTH_AND_RLS.md §8 erasure boundary.
  //   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY — Cloudflare Turnstile secret for siteverify (AECI-1457).
  //                 Read by `lib/turnstile.ts` to check the invisible widget's token on
  //                 in-app page-view beacons and on POST /api/page-views/turnstile.
  //                 Deploy-REQUIRED on every tier (ruling 2026-10-29): CI pushes it
  //                 from TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY_{STAGING,DEMO,PRODUCTION} and fails the
  //                 deploy without it. Absent (local) → no siteverify call, every
  //                 page_views.turnstile_verdict stays null, no metric. The web
  //                 Worker's public TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY var is the on/off switch,
  //                 required on every deployed tier. EU, EEA, UK and Swiss visitors
  //                 skip the check until the AECI-1458 counsel ruling (AECI-1581).
  //                 API Worker only.
  //   LINEAR_API_KEY — Linear personal API key for the form→Linear pipeline
  //                 (AECI-211 / Phase 6.4). Presented raw in `Authorization` (no
  //                 `Bearer`). Fail-open at RUNTIME: absent → issue creation is a
  //                 silent no-op and the request row sits open for the §6.7
  //                 reconciliation sweep. But since AECI-851 it is REQUIRED at
  //                 DEPLOY time on production — `promote-to-prod.yml` pushes it from
  //                 the un-suffixed `LINEAR_API_KEY` GH secret, `require-secrets.sh`
  //                 fails the promote before anything deploys when that secret is
  //                 empty, and `verify-worker-secrets.sh` re-asserts it on the live
  //                 Worker afterwards. Required rather than warn-and-skip because
  //                 the absent-key path emits NO metric and NO log, so a vendor
  //                 claim reaches nobody and the only signal is the 60-minute
  //                 reconciliation-sweep email. PRODUCTION ONLY: staging/demo/local
  //                 deliberately get no push, because the board constants
  //                 in `src/lib/linear.ts` point at the one shared "Vendor Requests"
  //                 project and a non-prod tier would file fixture claims there as
  //                 real issues. Bootstrap a new env by hand with `wrangler secret
  //                 put LINEAR_API_KEY --env <env>`.
  //   LINEAR_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET — HMAC-SHA256 signing secret for the inbound
  //                 Linear webhook (AECI-212 / Phase 6.5, `POST /api/webhooks/
  //                 linear`). Fail-CLOSED: absent → every delivery is rejected 401
  //                 before any write and `aeci.webhooks.linear.hmac_failure` fires,
  //                 so the Linear→site status sync stops but nothing is corrupted.
  //                 Pushed to production by `promote-to-prod.yml` (AECI-851) as
  //                 recommended/warn-and-skip, NOT required — a prod webhook may not
  //                 be registered in Linear at all, and its absence is loud (it has
  //                 its own alert) rather than silent like LINEAR_API_KEY above.
  //   RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET — Svix signing secret (`whsec_…`) for the inbound Resend
  //                 delivery webhook (AECI-1222, `POST /api/webhooks/resend`).
  //                 Fail-CLOSED: absent → every event 401s and
  //                 `aeci.webhooks.resend.signature_failure` counts it. Each tier's
  //                 Resend endpoint has its own secret, so CI pushes
  //                 RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET_{STAGING,DEMO,PRODUCTION} (deploy.yml /
  //                 promote-to-demo.yml / promote-to-prod.yml) under this one name.
  //                 REQUIRED on production since AECI-1438 (preflight + postflight);
  //                 warn-and-skip on staging and demo. Never on local dev or the web
  //                 Worker.
  //   STRIPE_SECRET_KEY — Stripe secret API key (AECI-1499, epic AECI-1339), read by
  //                 `src/lib/stripe.ts`. `sk_test_…` from the aeci-staging sandbox on
  //                 staging, the aeci-demo sandbox on demo, `sk_live_…` on
  //                 production. CI pushes STRIPE_SECRET_KEY_{STAGING,DEMO,PRODUCTION}
  //                 under this one name. Recommended + warn-and-skip on EVERY tier,
  //                 production included: absent → `stripeCheckoutAvailable` is false
  //                 and the buy, add and portal routes answer 503, which is the same
  //                 state STRIPE_CHECKOUT_ENABLED="false" gives production today.
  //                 Locally, a staging-sandbox `sk_test_…` in .dev.vars.
  //   STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET — signing secret (`whsec_…`) of the tier's Stripe webhook
  //                 endpoint, for `POST /api/webhooks/stripe`. Fail-CLOSED: absent →
  //                 every delivery 401s. Pushed from
  //                 STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET_{STAGING,DEMO,PRODUCTION}, recommended +
  //                 warn-and-skip on every tier. Locally it is the secret `stripe
  //                 listen` prints, not the dashboard endpoint's. API Worker only.
  //   RESEND_API_KEY — Resend API key for transactional email (AECI-240 / Phase 7.5,
  //                 §11.1). Read at runtime by `lib/email.ts` for review submit/
  //                 approve/reject confirmations, the account-deletion email, and the
  //                 reconcile-sweep admin alert. Optional + fail-open like
  //                 ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: absent (local) → every send is a silent
  //                 `'skipped'`, never blocking the action. CI pushes the one shared,
  //                 un-suffixed RESEND_API_KEY GH secret to staging, demo and production
  //                 (graceful, no hard gate). Pairs with the `EMAIL_FROM` var (sender).
  //                 See docs/email.md.
  //   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY — Supabase GoTrue Admin API key for the
  //                 split-identity seams (AECI-530 / ADR 0016 §6). Read at runtime
  //                 by lib/supabase-admin.ts — the ONLY module allowed to read it
  //                 (the single-module invariant) — for auth.users email reads
  //                 (seam #2), the GDPR erasure of the auth.users row (#3), and
  //                 vendor-claimant lookup + provisioning (#4a/#4b). The register
  //                 with each seam's endpoint and degrade behaviour is
  //                 docs/AUTH_AND_RLS.md §3.1. SINGLE shared un-suffixed GH secret —
  //                 one Supabase auth project backs every env (ADR 0017) — pushed
  //                 per env by CI (deploy.yml staging / promote-to-demo.yml /
  //                 promote-to-prod.yml, graceful warn-skip, no hard gate).
  //                 API WORKER ONLY: never the web Worker (which verifies user JWTs
  //                 with public JWKS material). It is the project-wide auth key
  //                 and the seams both delete and create identities on the
  //                 project that backs prod, so a local .dev.vars copy is live.
  //                 Optional + fail-open: absent (local) → reviewer emails
  //                 read null, the auth.users delete is skipped (the D1 erasure
  //                 still commits), and claim resolution reports 'unavailable'.
  //                 Rotating it invalidates every env at once — docs/CICD_PLAN.md §7.4.
  //
  // AECI-139: the API Worker also runs `scheduled` + `queue` handlers (daily
  // scheduled jobs). For a queue-backed job the cron `scheduled` handler does not
  // work inline — it enqueues a message onto the matching Cloudflare Queue, and the
  // `queue` consumer runs it (ADR 0013: cron→queue→consumer). Cron triggers AND the
  // queue producer/consumer bindings are registered ONLY on staging, demo and production (see
  // each env's `triggers` + `queues` blocks) — NOT on the base config, so local
  // dev carries no job queues and runs every job inline when its cron fires.
  // Twelve jobs have no queue in ANY env, by design (the reason is on each
  // `queueForJob` case in src/scheduled.ts), so they run inline on every tier and
  // log an info line. A queue-backed job with no binding on a deployed tier logs
  // the error `aeci.scheduled.queue_binding_missing` (AECI-1423). The
  // scheduled-job queues (`aeci-algolia-{sync,drift}-{env}` for AECI-139/140,
  // `aeci-stats-{env}` for the AECI-178 home-stats job, `aeci-reconcile-{env}` for the
  // AECI-214 request→Linear reconciliation sweep, `aeci-data-quality-{env}` for the
  // AECI-241 §23.1 suite, `aeci-attestation-notify-{env}` for the AECI-302 §7
  // attestation detector sweep, `aeci-vendor-snapshot-{env}` for the AECI-1210
  // per-vendor snapshot, `aeci-gsc-inspect-{env}` for the AECI-1236 URL Inspection
  // run, `aeci-traffic-classify-{env}` for the AECI-1345 traffic classification
  // writer, `aeci-stripe-reconcile-{env}` for the AECI-1503 Stripe reconcile, and
  // `aeci-brand-assets-{env}` for the AECI-1618 brand asset removal — eleven
  // per env across staging/demo/production) are
  // provisioned by an idempotent `wrangler queues create`
  // step in deploy.yml / promote-to-demo.yml / promote-to-prod.yml before the Worker
  // deploy.
  // AECI-1418 adds `aeci-ownership-sync-{env}`, which is NOT a scheduled-job queue:
  // routes produce onto it post-commit and this Worker consumes it (`queue()` routes
  // it by name). It is the one queue the base (local-dev) block also declares. PREREQS:
  // Workers Paid plan, and the CI CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN must carry the Queues edit
  // permission. See ADR 0013 and docs/environments.md.
  //
  // AECI-563 / ADR 0021: `POST /api/promote` never commits on the request. It validates,
  // starts an instance of the `PROMOTE_WORKFLOW` Cloudflare Workflow (class
  // `PromoteWorkflow`, re-exported from `src/index.ts` — wrangler resolves Workflow
  // classes off the Worker's main module), and returns `202 { jobId }`; the ingest runs
  // in the Workflow and `GET /api/promote/jobs/:id` serves the resulting IDs. The
  // instance id IS the caller-supplied job id, which is what makes a replayed kick-off
  // unable to commit twice. One Workflow per env (like the queues) so environments never
  // share instances. Workflows are created by `wrangler deploy` — no `queues create`-style
  // provisioning step — but they DO require the Workers Paid plan.
  //   PROMOTE_KV — companion namespace, own per env: `promote:payload:{jobId}` (24h)
  //                stages a bundle too large for the 1 MiB event-params cap, and
  //                `promote:result:{jobId}` (90d) mirrors the committed ID map so it
  //                outlives the 30-day instance retention. Absent → an oversize bundle
  //                is rejected 413 and the IDs are only fetchable while the instance
  //                lives. Keys + thresholds live in `src/lib/promote-jobs.ts`.
  //
  // AECI-773 / ADR 0026: TOKEN_RATE_LIMIT + WRITE_RATE_LIMIT — native Workers Rate
  // Limiting, the in-Worker layer BENEATH the zone's two WAF rate-limit rules. Those
  // rules count by client IP and cover only the Rule A and Rule B paths, so they
  // cannot guard a token-redemption or vendor-portal path, nor express §15.1's
  // "per authenticated user". Unlike every other binding in this file they need NO
  // provisioning: `namespace_id` is an integer you choose and there is no
  // `wrangler ratelimit create`. They are also NOT inherited into a named env, so the
  // block is repeated verbatim-but-for-ids in all four blocks. See `src/rate-limit-middleware.ts` and docs/waf-rate-limits.md §6.
  //
  // Cache purge (ADR 0020 §3): the promote handler and review moderation enqueue onto
  // `aeci-cache-purge-{env}` (producer binding CACHE_PURGE_QUEUE, in each env's
  // `queues.producers` below). The SSR Worker consumes it and calls
  // `ctx.cache.purge()`, which is entrypoint-scoped, so this Worker cannot purge the
  // SSR Worker's native cache itself. There is NO `WEB` service binding back to the
  // SSR Worker; the coupling is async, via the queue. The SSR Worker's
  // `POST /admin/purge` is the manual/incident + CI surface. When CACHE_PURGE_QUEUE is
  // unbound (local dev) the producers no-op, which is expected. See
  // docs/adr/0020-workers-cache-and-queue-purge.md and docs/CACHE_STRATEGY.md §5.
  "workers_dev": false,
  // Placement (AECI-839): ON in `env.production` only. Kept by ruling on 2026-11-02
  // unless evidence shows the location is wrong (docs/RUNBOOKS.md step 0b).
  // The production D1 primary is in ENAM since 2026-10-05. Read replication is on,
  // but replicas go inactive between requests in sparse regions, so a Worker in
  // the visitor's colo still pays 2-3 serial rounds to ENAM per render.
  // Targeted placement to `aws:us-east-1` runs the Worker in the Cloudflare data
  // center nearest that region. Cloudflare does not publish the D1 primary's exact
  // colo, so Virginia is the best guess. A wrong guess within ENAM costs tens of ms
  // per round, not the ~850 ms SIN pays today. Chosen over `mode: "smart"`: no
  // 15 min analysis, no 1% unplaced control traffic, no chance the analyzer
  // declines to place. Staging, demo and local stay at the edge. The SSR Worker is
  // never placed: it serves assets and renders through the named `Renderer`
  // entrypoint over the `ctx.exports` loopback, which placement does not route.
  // Traces run at 100% on every tier because Cloudflare samples at the head and cannot
  // keep only the slow requests, so a rare stall would be missed at any lower rate.
  // A deploy applies this block, so removing it turns tracing off. `redact_query_string`
  // drops the query string from request URLs in logs and traces (AECI-1594): the Privacy
  // Policy's "Debugging record" promises link parameters are removed. `invocation_logs`
  // stays false on every tier: Cloudflare's per-request record stores the raw IP headers.
  // Turning it on is a debugging window (Privacy Policy, AECI-1656), never a config edit.
  // `docs/OBSERVABILITY.md`.
  "observability": {
    "redact_query_string": true,
    "logs": {
      "enabled": true,
      "invocation_logs": false,
    },
    "traces": { "enabled": true, "head_sampling_rate": 1 },
  },
  // THE TOP-LEVEL BLOCK IS THE LOCAL-DEV CONFIG (AECI-1268). The per-PR preview
  // tier and its `env.preview` block retired on 2026-10-10; `pnpm dev:bound` /
  // `dev:agent` now run this block with no `--env`. Nothing deploys it: every CI
  // deploy passes `--env staging|demo|production`. Its resource names still say
  // `preview` (`aeci-app-preview`, `aeci-uploads-preview`, the two KV ids, the
  // `aeci-promote-preview` Workflow). They are LOCAL-ONLY names now, kept so the
  // existing `.wrangler/state` (local D1 keyed by `database_id`, local R2 keyed by
  // bucket name, local KV keyed by id) carries over untouched. The remote
  // resources they once named are deleted by the operator steps in
  // docs/environments.md §"Operator steps after merge (AECI-1268)".
  "vars": {
    // `development` is the local label. Algolia folds it onto `staging_*` for
    // READS; every index write is refused locally unless ALGOLIA_ALLOW_LOCAL_WRITES
    // is "true" in .dev.vars (src/lib/algolia-write-guard.ts).
    "ENV": "development",
    // AECI-74: overridden at dev/deploy time via `wrangler --var COMMIT_SHA:<sha> --var DEPLOYED_AT:<iso>`.
    "COMMIT_SHA": "unknown",
    "DEPLOYED_AT": "1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
    // AECI-1454 (epic AECI-1413): the ownership record schema the consumer sends to the
    // review app. "1" here: a receiver that does not parse v2 answers 400, and a 400
    // is permanent, so "2" before this tier's receiver parses v2 drops every message.
    "REVIEW_APP_MIRROR_SCHEMA": "1",
    // AECI-193 / ADR 0017: Supabase base URL (public) — the SINGLE shared
    // auth project across all environments. Drives JWKS user-JWT
    // verification in lib/user-auth.ts. The anon key is never on the API
    // Worker; the service-role key is a Worker SECRET (not a var here), used
    // only by the ADR-0016 split-identity seams (AUTH_AND_RLS.md §3.1).
    "SUPABASE_URL": "https://ktuhnlypztujpsseujzx.supabase.co",
    // AECI-640 / AECI-642: PostHog INGEST host + publishable project key.
    // `us.i.posthog.com` is the INGEST host; `us.posthog.com` (no `.i`) is the
    // MANAGEMENT API and 404s on an intake path. Easy to swap, confusing to
    // diagnose. The OTLP intakes (`/i/v1/logs`, `/i/v1/metrics`) authenticate
    // with an `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header; an `?api_key=` query param
    // is rejected 401 there.
    //
    // POSTHOG_PROJECT_KEY is a `phc_` PUBLISHABLE key. The SSR Worker already
    // renders it into the served HTML on every page (window.__AECI_POSTHOG__),
    // so it is not a secret. A committed var has no provisioning step to forget;
    // a CI-pushed secret that warn-and-skips can leave a tier dark (AECI-326).
    //
    // Project topology (POSTHOG_MIGRATION_SPEC.md §3.6 / D4): local, staging
    // and demo all report to `aec-integrations-dev` (525793); ONLY
    // production reports to `aec-integrations` (354071). Tiers inside the
    // non-prod project are separated by `$host`/`env`; local dev reports
    // `env=development`.
    "POSTHOG_HOST": "https://us.i.posthog.com",
    "POSTHOG_PROJECT_KEY": "phc_pY8FDQ9FNAmFUJFFd85jNKAAUCMvndUsyGMMV8TR97MD",
    // AECI-1499 (epic AECI-1339): Stripe. Non-secret ids only; the API key and the
    // webhook secret are Worker secrets (see the secrets block above). Customer
    // Portal configurations, one per interval, chosen by `stripePortalConfigFor`
    // (src/lib/stripe.ts). Both configs have quantity update and plan switching
    // OFF; both cancel at period end only (annual since AECI-1516). Prices are
    // resolved at runtime by lookup key (`managed_annual`, `managed_monthly`), so
    // no price id is configured. STRIPE_CHECKOUT_ENABLED gates the buy, add and
    // portal routes through `stripeCheckoutAvailable` (AECI-1339 ruling,
    // 2026-10-28): "true" on local and staging. Production stays "false" until
    // the switch-on, and demo is "false" too while it mirrors production.
    // Local dev uses the aeci-staging sandbox.
    "STRIPE_PORTAL_CONFIG_ANNUAL": "bpc_1USUHUQn1Dn3MGrP1bksUZFx",
    "STRIPE_PORTAL_CONFIG_MONTHLY": "bpc_1USUHpQn1Dn3MGrPnsBlzugb",
    "STRIPE_CHECKOUT_ENABLED": "true",
    // AECI-1553: the private row threads release switch (STAGE_2_ATTESTATIONS_SPEC.md
    // §15.16). Only "true" turns them on; absent or anything else is off, and every
    // vendor thread route answers 404. "true" on local dev only. Staging, demo and
    // production stay "false" until Chris turns threads on (ruled 2026-10-31). Read by `threadsEnabled`
    // (src/lib/claim-threads.ts).
    "THREADS_ENABLED": "true",
  },
  "dev": {
    "port": 8787,
  },
  // AECI-54: TAXONOMY_KV powers `GET /api/taxonomy` read-through caching with
  // a 5-minute TTL. Each environment has its own namespace so staging never
  // poisons production. The ids here are the retired preview tier's namespaces
  // (`aeci-api-taxonomy-preview`, `aeci-api-promote-preview`), kept as LOCAL-ONLY
  // ids so local KV state carries over (AECI-1268); per-env blocks below override
  // with each env's own namespace ID. Handler at `src/routes/taxonomy.ts` falls back to a
  // direct D1 read when the binding is absent, so this stays optional.
  "kv_namespaces": [
    {
      "binding": "TAXONOMY_KV",
      "id": "9acd62934424496da76de31aa761255f",
      "preview_id": "9acd62934424496da76de31aa761255f",
    },
    {
      "binding": "PROMOTE_KV",
      "id": "48be5fd8a6284d6bb7b43b2f3aac323d",
      "preview_id": "48be5fd8a6284d6bb7b43b2f3aac323d",
    },
  ],
  // AECI-773 / ADR 0026: native Workers Rate Limiting, the in-Worker layer BENEATH
  // the zone's two WAF rate-limit rules. Those rules count per client IP and cover
  // only the Rule A and Rule B paths (docs/waf-rate-limits.md §1), so token-redemption
  // and vendor-portal paths have no edge rule, and no WAF rule here expresses §15.1's
  // "per authenticated user". These bindings have neither limitation.
  //
  // This block is NOT inherited into a named environment, so it is repeated in all
  // three env blocks below. A bucket declared only here is bound on ZERO deployed
  // Workers. `src/wrangler-ratelimits.spec.ts` asserts all four blocks and their values.
  //
  // Every (bucket, env) pair gets its OWN namespace_id: counters are shared
  // ACCOUNT-WIDE by namespace_id, across Workers, and the sibling
  // aec-integrations-review app already ships this binding on the same account.
  // The ids avoid the 1001/1002 from the Cloudflare docs example, which a
  // neighbouring Worker is the most likely to hold. The base block is the local-dev
  // config (AECI-1268), so these ids only ever count local requests.
  "ratelimits": [
    {
      "name": "TOKEN_RATE_LIMIT",
      "namespace_id": "7731",
      "simple": { "limit": 10, "period": 10 },
    },
    {
      "name": "WRITE_RATE_LIMIT",
      "namespace_id": "7735",
      "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 60 },
    },
  ],
  // AECI-1418: the ownership-sync queue, the one queue the local-dev block declares.
  // Queue bindings are not inherited into a named env, and this message has no inline
  // fallback, so local dev runs it through a local (Miniflare) queue: a local claim
  // approval enqueues, the local consumer delivers to REVIEW_APP_OWNERSHIP_URL from
  // .dev.vars, or writes an `unconfigured` ledger row when it is unset. The name is
  // local-only; nothing deploys this block.
  "queues": {
    "producers": [{ "queue": "aeci-ownership-sync-local", "binding": "OWNERSHIP_SYNC_QUEUE" }],
    "consumers": [
      {
        "queue": "aeci-ownership-sync-local",
        "max_batch_size": 1,
        "max_retries": 5,
        "max_concurrency": 1,
      },
    ],
  },
  // AECI-563 / ADR 0021: the promote ingest Workflow. The base block is the local-dev
  // config (`wrangler dev` serves the Workflow locally under this LOCAL-ONLY name);
  // per-env blocks below give each environment its own Workflow so instance ids,
  // which ARE the caller-supplied job ids, never collide across tiers.
  "workflows": [
    {
      "name": "aeci-promote-preview",
      "binding": "PROMOTE_WORKFLOW",
      "class_name": "PromoteWorkflow",
    },
  ],
  // AECI-1228: the two archive entries were added by `wrangler r2 bucket create`. No
  // code reads these binding names, and nothing deploys this block. The archive the
  // retention job writes is PAGE_VIEWS_ARCHIVE, bound on staging and production only.
  "r2_buckets": [
    { "binding": "UPLOADS", "bucket_name": "aeci-uploads-preview" },
    {
      "binding": "aeci_page_views_archive_staging",
      "bucket_name": "aeci-page-views-archive-staging",
    },
    {
      "binding": "aeci_page_views_archive_production",
      "bucket_name": "aeci-page-views-archive-production",
    },
  ],
  // ADR 0016 / AECI-252: the application database is Cloudflare D1, bound as `DB`
  // and reached via the Drizzle client factory `getDb(env)` (`src/db/client.ts`).
  // `wrangler dev` serves a LOCAL SQLite copy (in `.wrangler/state`), keyed by
  // `database_id`. Keep this id: changing it orphans every developer's local
  // database. The remote `aeci-app-preview` it once named retires with the preview
  // tier (AECI-1268), so a `--remote` call against this block fails once the
  // operator deletes it, which is the intent. Drizzle writes flat
  // migration SQL to `apps/api/migrations/` (drizzle.config.ts `out`), which is
  // wrangler's default `migrations_dir`, so `wrangler d1 migrations apply` finds
  // them with no `migrations_pattern` override.
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "aeci-app-preview",
      "database_id": "40b2021f-9023-4f4a-affb-9d2c657bb7e3",
      "migrations_dir": "migrations",
    },
  ],
  "env": {
    "staging": {
      "name": "aeci-api-staging",
      "workers_dev": false,
      "vars": {
        "ENV": "staging",
        // AECI-1228: archive page_views days at 2 days old, not 371, so the archive
        // path runs every night on staging instead of first running in June 2027.
        "PAGE_VIEWS_ARCHIVE_AGE_DAYS": "2",
        // AECI-74: overridden at deploy time via `wrangler --var COMMIT_SHA:<sha> --var DEPLOYED_AT:<iso>`.
        "COMMIT_SHA": "unknown",
        "DEPLOYED_AT": "1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
        // AECI-1454 (epic AECI-1413): the ownership record schema the consumer sends to the
        // review app. "1" here: a receiver that does not parse v2 answers 400, and a 400
        // is permanent, so "2" before this tier's receiver parses v2 drops every message.
        "REVIEW_APP_MIRROR_SCHEMA": "1",
        // AECI-193 / ADR 0017: Supabase base URL (public) — the SINGLE shared
        // auth project across all environments. Drives JWKS user-JWT
        // verification in lib/user-auth.ts. The anon key is never on the API
        // Worker; the service-role key is a Worker SECRET (not a var here), used
        // only by the ADR-0016 split-identity seams (AUTH_AND_RLS.md §3.1).
        "SUPABASE_URL": "https://ktuhnlypztujpsseujzx.supabase.co",
        "POSTHOG_HOST": "https://us.i.posthog.com",
        // See the top-level vars note (AECI-640): non-prod tiers report to
        // `aec-integrations-dev` (525793).
        "POSTHOG_PROJECT_KEY": "phc_pY8FDQ9FNAmFUJFFd85jNKAAUCMvndUsyGMMV8TR97MD",
        // AECI-240 / Phase 7.5: transactional email (Resend). EMAIL_FROM is the
        // verified sender; PUBLIC_SITE_URL builds absolute links in emails.
        // The RESEND_API_KEY secret is pushed by CI (graceful) — absent → sends skip.
        "EMAIL_FROM": "AEC Integrations <notifications@aecintegrations.com>",
        // EMAIL_BCC (a blind copy of every send to support) is unset on every tier
        // since AECI-1220. The send ledger records each send. To turn it back on,
        // add `"EMAIL_BCC": "support@aecintegrations.com"` here. See docs/email.md.
        "PUBLIC_SITE_URL": "https://staging.aecintegrations.com",
        // AECI-1220: the support inbox, the ONE recipient for every operator email.
        // Keep it to one address. Which sends go out on which tier is `envRule` in
